_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 38
---
order: 38
---
short_label: I.38
---
statement: Triangles which are on equal bases and in the same parallels equal one another.
---
proof:

Let *ABC* and *DEF* be triangles on equal bases *BC* and *EF* and in the same parallels *BF* and *AD.*

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="310" height="210" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.38 diagram" src="propI38.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    geomlib.init({
        canvasid: "canvas_0",
        background: "35,19,100",
        title: "I.38",
        elements: [
            "B;point;free;60,170",
            "C;point;free;130,170",
            "A;point;free;120,40",
            "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "AD';line;parallel;A,B,C;0;0;0",
            "D;point;lineSlider;AD',150,40",
            "E;point;lineSlider;B,C,190,170",
            "F;point;parallelogram;C,B,E",
            "DEF;polygon;triangle;D,E,F;0;0;0,0,0;random",
            "AD;line;connect;A,D",
            "CE;line;connect;C,E",
            "G;point;parallelogram;A,C,B",
            "GA;line;connect;G,A",
            "GB;line;connect;G,B",
            "H;point;parallelogram;D,E,F",
            "HD;line;connect;H,D",
            "HF;line;connect;H,F"
        ],
    });
</script>
</figure>

I say that the triangle *ABC* equals the triangle *DEF.*

Produce *AD* in both directions to *G* and *H.* Draw *BG* through *B* parallel to *CA,* and draw *FH* through *F* parallel to *DE.* [!just I.Post.2; I.31]

Then each of the figures *GBCA* and *DEFH* is a parallelogram, and *GBCA* equals *DEFH,* for they are on equal bases *BC* and *EF* and in the same parallels *BF* and *GH.* [!just I.36]

Moreover the triangle *ABC* is half of the parallelogram *GBCA,* for the diameter *AB* bisects it. And the triangle *FED* is half of the parallelogram *DEFH,* for the diameter *DF* bisects it. [!just I.34]

[!just C.N.]

Therefore the triangle *ABC* equals the triangle *DEF.*

Therefore *triangles which are on equal bases and in the same parallels equal one another.*

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
---
body:

The idea of the argument is clear: since parallelograms on equal bases and in the same parallels are equal by @I.36, and the triangles are half the parallelograms by @I.34, therefore the triangles are also equal.

#### Use of Proposition 38

This proposition is used in @I.40, @I.42, and @VI.1.
